# Target C — audited train-closure rank, host anchoring, and two-sided bracing §§151–152

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**Lifecycle:** audited-working proof-only route module; theorem-state authority unchanged.  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**

This module consolidates the exact structural content formerly spread across the
face-closure / train-bracing research branches. All rigidity matrices below are on the
connected nonisolated positive-contact core. Contact-isolated source points remain in
the full source and are never inserted into the core affine kernel.

No response-conductance theorem, KKT coefficient floor, or carrier decimal is used.

---

# §151 — exceptional-face closure corank and stopping-host expansion

Let `G=(V,C)` be the connected nonisolated positive-contact core, with

\[
v=|V|,\qquad e=|C|.
\]

Let `H` be the full actual diameter graph after the C65 normalization, `h=|H|`, and

\[
s_Z=e+h-(2v-2)\ge0.
\]

Let `R` be the number of bounded strict-rhombus faces, `f` the number of bounded
nonquadrilateral faces, and `T` the number of complete stopped trains. The audited
stopped-train decomposition gives

\[
T=e-2R=h-s_Z+2f.
\tag{151.1}
\]

## 151.1 Train angular rates and the face-closure matrix

For an oriented unit contact edge `ij`, write `r_{ij}=p_j-p_i`. Any contact
infinitesimal flex satisfies

\[
u_j-u_i=\omega_{ij}Jr_{ij}
\]

for a unique scalar `omega_{ij}`. On a strict rhombus, differentiating boundary closure
forces equal angular rates on opposite sides, so the rate is constant along each
complete train. Write it `omega_tau`.

Conversely, assign one scalar to each train and prescribe

\[
\dot r_e=\omega_{\tau(e)}Jr_e.
\]

Strict-rhombus faces close automatically. For each bounded nonquadrilateral face `F`,
with its occurrence-counted oriented boundary, impose

\[
\sum_{e\in\partial F}\omega_{\tau(e)}Jr_e=0.
\tag{151.2}
\]

The two scalar coordinates of these `f` vector equations define

\[
C_{face}:\mathbb R^T\to\mathbb R^{2f}.
\]

Bounded face boundaries span the real cycle space of a connected plane graph even with
outer-face bridges or articulation vertices. Hence the prescribed edge derivatives
integrate path-independently exactly when (151.2) holds. Therefore, modulo translations,

\[
\ker R_C/\mathrm{Trans}\cong\ker C_{face}.
\tag{151.3}
\]

The all-ones train-rate vector is global infinitesimal rotation.

## 151.2 Exact corank/self-stress identity

Put

\[
\sigma_C:=\dim\ker R_C^T.
\]

From (151.3),

\[
\dim\ker R_C=T-\operatorname{rank}C_{face}+2.
\]

Thus

\[
\sigma_C
=e-\operatorname{rank}R_C
=e-2v+T-\operatorname{rank}C_{face}+2.
\]

Euler gives

\[
v-e+(R+f+1)=2,
\]

and `T=e-2R`; substituting yields the exact identity

\[
\boxed{
2f-\operatorname{rank}C_{face}=\sigma_C.
}
\tag{151.4}
\]

Canonical mixed rigidity has rank `2v-3`, so the mixed contact+diameter stress space has
dimension `s_Z+1`. Every pure contact stress embeds in it by zero diameter coordinates.
The normalized KKT stress has nonzero diameter part, so it supplies one mixed stress
outside the pure-contact subspace. Hence

\[
\boxed{
0\le\sigma_C\le s_Z,
\qquad
2f-s_Z\le\operatorname{rank}C_{face}\le2f.
}
\tag{151.5}
\]

In particular, `s_Z=0` forces full row rank.

## 151.3 Hall expansion for every defect-face set

For any set `S` of bounded nonquadrilateral faces, let `C_S` be the `2|S|` rows of
`C_face` belonging to those faces, and let `N(S)` be the set of complete trains having
a noncancelling terminal occurrence on at least one face of `S`.

Any row dependence of `C_S` extends by zero to a row dependence of `C_face`, so

\[
2|S|-\operatorname{rank}C_S\le\sigma_C.
\]

Since `C_S` is supported on at most `|N(S)|` columns,

\[
\boxed{
|N(S)|\ge2|S|-\sigma_C\ge2|S|-s_Z.
}
\tag{151.6}
\]

This is a finite local expansion theorem; it is not merely the global train count.

## 151.4 Outer-to-outer train capacity

Let `L_{OO}` be the number of complete trains whose two terminal occurrences lie on the
outer face. These are zero columns of `C_face`. Conversely, the audited no-bounded-return
theorem excludes a nontrivial train returning to one bounded face, and bounded singleton
loops are excluded by the endpoint geometry; thus a train with a bounded endpoint has
a nonzero bounded-face column.

Therefore

\[
\operatorname{rank}C_{face}\le T-L_{OO}.
\]

Using (151.1), (151.4), and `sigma_C<=s_Z`,

\[
\boxed{L_{OO}\le h.}
\tag{151.7}
\]

If `f>0`, the coordinate subspace supported only on the `L_{OO}` zero columns does not
contain the all-trains rotation vector. It therefore gives `L_{OO}` independent
non-Euclidean contact flexes. Mixed rigidity maps these injectively into the `h`
diameter first-variation coordinates, while KKT stationarity places the image in the
codimension-one hyperplane perpendicular to the positive diameter multiplier vector.
Hence

\[
\boxed{f>0\Longrightarrow L_{OO}\le h-1.}
\tag{151.8}
\]

For `f=0`, rotation lies in the outer-train coordinate subspace and only (151.7) is
valid.

## 151.5 Grounded stopping-host direction network

Collapse each complete stopped train to its two stopping-face terminal occurrences,
with the outer face grounded. For a train joining distinct bounded hosts `F,G`, choose a
coherent projective train direction `u_tau`. The two endpoint contributions have
opposite physical boundary orientations, so after orienting the host edge its column
has blocks

\[
+Ju_\tau\text{ at }F,
\qquad
-Ju_\tau\text{ at }G.
\]

A bounded-to-outer train has one bounded block. Thus a row-dual vector
`(y_F)_{F}` lies in `ker C_face^T` exactly when

\[
(y_F-y_G)\cdot Ju_\tau=0
\tag{151.9}
\]

for every bounded-bounded host train, and

\[
y_F\cdot Ju_\tau=0
\tag{151.10}
\]

for every bounded-to-outer train.

Delete the outer host and let `K_1,...,K_c` be the connected bounded-host components.
For component `K_j`, let `U_j` be the projective directions of its outer incident
trains and put

\[
r_j:=\dim\operatorname{span}\{Ju:u\in U_j\}\in\{0,1,2\}.
\]

Assigning a constant vector to all face variables in one component gives a transpose-
kernel subspace of dimension `2-r_j`. Distinct components have disjoint row support.
Therefore

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{j=1}^c(2-r_j)
\le\dim\ker C_{face}^T
=\sigma_C
\le s_Z.
}
\tag{151.11}
\]

Consequences:

- an unanchored bounded-host component costs two units of `s_Z`;
- a component whose outer trains are all projectively parallel costs one unit; and
- if `s_Z=0`, every bounded-host component has outer anchors in at least two distinct
  projective directions.

This is exact. It does **not** turn asymptotic corridor phase purity into exact rank
loss: a small transverse anchor population can retain full rank.

**§151 hostile-audit disposition:** PASS. The exact corank identity, Hall expansion,
outer-train capacity, and host-anchor deficiency all follow on the core without deleting
contact isolates or assuming pure contact stress vanishes.

