# §152 — response-free two-sided diameter bracing of outer trains

Let `tau` be a complete stopped train whose two terminal occurrences lie on the actual
outer face. The audited train primitive supplies a Jordan shore split

\[
V=S\sqcup T
\]

whose contact cut is exactly the train edge set. Choose the unit train vector `u` so
every cut contact oriented from `T` to `S` has vector `u`.

## 152.1 A projective diameter direction is unique

If two diameter pairs in any finite planar set of diameter `D` are parallel, orient
them in the same direction:

\[
b-a=Du,
\qquad d-c=Du.
\]

Put `v=c-a`. Diameter boundedness gives

\[
|Du-v|\le D,
\qquad |Du+v|\le D.
\]

Squaring and adding yields

\[
2D^2+2|v|^2\le2D^2,
\]

so `v=0`. Thus the two unordered diameter pairs are identical.

## 152.2 KKT cut resultant

Let the relative-interior KKT contact weights be `alpha_e>0` and actual diameter weights
`beta_h>0`, and put

\[
A_\tau:=\sum_{e\in E(\tau)}\alpha_e>0.
\]

Sum equilibrium over the vertices of `S`. Internal forces cancel, leaving

\[
\boxed{
A_\tau u
=
\sum_{\substack{h=xy\in H\\x\in T,\ y\in S}}
\beta_h(p_y-p_x).
}
\tag{152.1}
\]

At least one actual diameter edge crosses the cut. If `r_h=p_y-p_x`, dot with `Ju`:

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{h\pitchfork\tau}\beta_h(r_h\cdot Ju)=0.
}
\tag{152.2}
\]

## 152.3 Both transverse signs are mandatory

Suppose the numbers `r_h\cdot Ju` do not contain both positive and negative values.
Positivity of every `beta_h` and (152.2) then force

\[
r_h\cdot Ju=0
\]

for every crossing diameter. Hence all crossing diameters are parallel to `u`, and the
projective uniqueness lemma leaves one unordered pair. Equation (152.1) forces its
oriented vector to be `Du`.

Use the exact finite one-train shear: keep shore `T` fixed and translate the full-source
shore `S` by

\[
\Delta_\theta=R_\theta u-u.
\]

All old contacts are preserved exactly. The unique crossing diameter changes from
`Du` to `Du+Delta_theta`, whose squared length is

\[
\begin{aligned}
|Du+\Delta_\theta|^2
&=D^2+2D(\cos\theta-1)+2(1-\cos\theta)\\
&=D^2-2(D-1)(1-\cos\theta)
<D^2
\end{aligned}
\tag{152.3}
\]

for sufficiently small nonzero `theta` and `D>1`.

Every same-shore diameter is unchanged. Every old strict noncontact remains longer than
one and every old nondiameter pair remains shorter than `D` for sufficiently small
`theta` by finite slack. Thus the full source remains unit-separated and has diameter
at most `D`, with the same cardinality and all old contacts.

If a same-shore diameter remains, the result is another global diameter minimizer with
strictly fewer actual diameter pairs and unchanged contact set, contradicting the
lexicographic normalization. If none remains, the global diameter has strictly
decreased, contradicting minimality.

Therefore every outer-to-outer train has two crossing actual diameter pairs
`h_+,h_-` with

\[
\boxed{
r_{h_+}\cdot Ju>0,
\qquad
r_{h_-}\cdot Ju<0.
}
\tag{152.4}
\]

In particular, each outer train is crossed by at least two distinct actual diameter
edges. This is a signed geometric strengthening of the capacity bounds (151.7)–(151.8).

The theorem uses no response conductance or weighted second-curvature argument.

**§152 hostile-audit disposition:** PASS. The shore shear is a full-source finite
competitor; contact isolates are moved with their geometric shore rather than deleted.

---

# Remaining obstruction

The module does not close C-II. One diameter edge may cross many train cuts, and a
bounded stopping-host direction network can satisfy the Hall expansion. The live
full-core problem is to combine §§151–152 with phase/corridor geometry or exact metric
train coordinates strongly enough to force an excess of independent closure motions,
an outer-train capacity violation, or a simultaneous diameter descent.

The macroscopic inactive-container branch remains separate and open.

**Overall verdict:** PASS at audited-working proof-only scope. Canonical theorem-state
authority is unchanged; C-II and Level I remain open.
